 "semantics": "ical.net (Ical.Net.Tests/RecurrenceTests.cs ParseTestCaseFile/ExecuteRecurrenceTestCase): each block becomes a CalendarEvent whose DTSTART is new CalDateTime(value, \"UTC\") (DATE values stay all-day), RRULE as given, optional DURATION; occurrences = evt.GetOccurrences(UTC, startAt) and their start values are compared with INSTANCES (also parsed as UTC). Everything is evaluated in UTC, so the floating DATE-TIME values copied here are equivalent to UTC wall-clock. The evaluator does NOT emit a DTSTART that does not match the rule (e.g. FREQ=HOURLY;BYHOUR=0;BYYEARDAY=360,-2 from 20251220T000000 starts at 20251226T000000), so include_dtstart=false. START-AT: occurrences are requested from that instant and the harness filters INSTANCES to start >= START-AT; that filter has been applied to 'expected' here and START-AT is recorded as between.start (inclusive, no end). EXCEPTION blocks: error=true; the comment gives the .NET exception and the step at which it must be thrown: Construction (default: parsing the RRULE), GetOccurrenceInvocation, or Enumeration (enumerating occurrences; used for ical.net's year-9999 DateTime limit and for unbounded evaluation, which are ical.net-specific rather than RFC 5545 requirements). Blocks without DTSTART only test RRULE parsing; their dtstart is a placeholder (see comment). The upstream harness also re-runs every case with each instance as DTSTART (COUNT reduced by the index).",
